# Development

Initialize the module with `go mod init {{project_slug}}`, or use the intended
remote module path and replace `{{project_slug}}/internal/` in the Go imports.
Resolve the dependencies (import paths, so the `/v2` majors stay) before
running the Makefile:

```sh
go get charm.land/bubbletea/v2 charm.land/bubbles/v2 charm.land/lipgloss/v2 golang.org/x/term
go get github.com/stretchr/testify github.com/charmbracelet/x/exp/teatest/v2
go mod tidy
make install
```

Install golangci-lint using its official instructions and write its installed
version to `.golangci-lint-version`. Run `make help` for every target.

```sh
make fmt-check tidy-check vet lint build test test-integration smoke coverage
go test -race -run TestFrames/detail ./internal/ui/
```

## Trying the UI

Run `./bin/{{project_slug}}` yourself, in a real terminal. Coding agents
don't: their shell has no terminal, so the binary prints text, and wrapping it
in `script` or `tmux` hangs the session. The Claude Code hook denies those
wrappers. Agents prove UI behavior through `make test`.

## Golden frames

`internal/ui/testdata/TestFrames/*.golden` hold each screen's exact `View()`
output, ANSI styles included, at 60x16. A view change, or a Bubbles or Lip Gloss
release that changes default styles, fails `TestFrames` with a diff. Rewrite
them with `make golden` and read the diff before committing; never hand-edit
them.

`make test`, `make test-integration` and `make coverage` use `-race`, which
needs cgo and a C compiler.
